      "narrative": "Global institutional asset allocators and multi-asset hedge funds have initiated a decisive rotation across international capital markets, moving billions of dollars out of richly valued US artificial intelligence infrastructure equities and redirecting capital toward undervalued domestic-focused Asian equities, with India and China emerging as the prime beneficiaries. For nearly three years, the dominant global macro trade concentrated in US hyperscalers (Microsoft, Alphabet, Amazon, Meta) and semiconductor hardware manufacturers (Nvidia, Broadcom). However, institutional sentiment has shifted sharply following the US midterm elections and growing Wall Street skepticism regarding the commercial return on investment (ROI) from massive datacenter capital expenditures.\n\nThe catalyst for this rotation is the emerging reality that enterprise AI monetization timelines are stretching from an anticipated 12–18 months to 4–5 years. Simultaneously, US datacenter expansion is confronting severe physical bottlenecks, including multi-year utility grid interconnection delays, environmental permitting resistance, and rising commercial power tariffs. In response, global portfolio managers are reallocating capital away from tech names trading at 35–45x forward earnings and seeking refuge in real-economy, cash-generative equities characterized by resilient domestic demand, attractive dividend yields, and reasonable valuations.\n\nIndia's equity markets have positioned themselves as a primary magnet for this rotating capital. While US tech multiples expanded, India’s domestic macroeconomic backdrop strengthened: domestic consumer spending surged during the festive Dhanteras and Diwali shopping window, global crude oil prices eased below $75 per barrel (reducing India's import bill and inflation pressures), and Indian corporate and banking balance sheets sit at multi-decade lows in bad loan ratios. Foreign institutional investors (FIIs), who had pulled capital from emerging markets earlier this year, reversed course to accumulate Indian financials, healthcare leaders, industrial manufacturers, and domestic retail giants.\n\nThis macro rotation has profound corporate strategy implications across emerging Asian economies. The influx of institutional liquidity is re-rating Indian mid-market capital costs downward, opening attractive windows for domestic initial public offerings (IPOs), secondary equity raises, and infrastructure funding. At the same time, it is compelling corporate leaders across technology and industrial sectors to re-evaluate their capital budgeting, shifting away from speculative, multi-year AI infrastructure investments and prioritizing core, high-return domestic capacity expansion.",
